# The Girl They Called Aspirin

Everybody called her Aspirin -- she always looked like she was aspiring to become a special state of being, and just looking at her made your headache go away.

Nobody remembered exactly when the nickname started. Some said it was old Mr. Kowalski at the pharmacy, who claimed that watching her walk past his window each morning was better than any pill he could prescribe. Others swore it was the third-grade teacher, Mrs. Delacroix, who once told the class that Aspirin had "the kind of face that dissolves troubles." Whatever the origin, the name stuck like a label on a bottle, and somehow it fit her the way a glove fits a hand -- perfectly, and without anyone quite understanding why.

She was not beautiful in the way magazines define beauty. Her nose was a little too broad, her hair refused to hold any style for more than an hour, and she had a habit of squinting at the world as though it were a math problem she was determined to solve. But there was something about her -- a stillness, perhaps, or a kind of quiet attention -- that made people feel better just by standing near her.

The town learned to use her like medicine.

When the Hendersons' marriage was falling apart, they found themselves both showing up at the same time to watch Aspirin tend her garden, and somehow they ended up talking -- really talking -- for the first time in months. When old Mrs. Petrov lost her son, she would sit on Aspirin's porch steps and say nothing at all, and Aspirin would say nothing back, and the silence between them was warmer than any words of condolence. When the boys came home from the war with things they couldn't speak about, they would find excuses to help her carry groceries, and she would let them, and never once ask them what was wrong.

She never charged for any of this, of course. You cannot charge for being a window that lets the light in.

But here is what nobody in town knew, what nobody thought to ask: Aspirin herself had headaches. Terrible ones, in fact, that arrived without warning and pressed against her skull like thumbs. She had learned to live with them the way you live with weather -- enduring, adjusting, never quite expecting them but never quite surprised either. And she had learned something strange in all those years of absorbing other people's pain: that the cure for a headache is not always a pill. Sometimes it is the act of being useful. Sometimes it is watching someone else's face relax because you were there.

So she kept tending her garden and sitting on her porch and letting the broken people of the town orbit around her like moths who had mistaken her for something gentler than a flame. And every evening, when the last visitor had gone home, she would sit alone in her kitchen with a cold cloth pressed to her forehead, and she would smile -- not because the pain was gone, but because she understood something that took most people a lifetime to learn:

That we are all, in the end, somebody's remedy. And that being taken -- being needed, being swallowed whole by other people's sorrows -- is not the same as being used. Sometimes it is the only way any of us dissolves into the water of the world and finally does some good.

The town never learned her real name. It didn't matter. By the time she died -- quietly, in her sleep, at the age of eighty-nine -- half the people at her funeral couldn't tell you what she had ever actually said to them. But every single one of them could tell you how she had made them feel.

Better. That was the word they kept using. Just better.

And in the end, isn't that the only prescription that ever really matters? [end of text]


